{
  "id": "Compound::DB00401 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Nisoldipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is linked to Cyp3a4 through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Nisoldipine, as part of the dihydropyridine class, binds to Cyp3a4 via shared ontological relationships. Pathways such as Nisoldipine → Dihydropyridines → Cyp3a4 are consistent, with multiple lowest common ancestors reinforcing the connection through different molecular mechanisms. Additionally, Nisoldipine is associated with antihypertensive agents, which in turn bind to Cyp3a4, further supporting the relationship. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism where Nisoldipine, through its class and pharmacological properties, influences Cyp3a4 activity. The evidence is consistent across different pathways, suggesting a general role of dihydropyridines in modulating Cyp3a4, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
